n no normalised distribution name matching `cuda-*`, `nvidia-*`, or `triton`, and `torch.version.cuda` MUST be `None`. **Three further constraints on both lockfiles, all found by building them (AM-65, AM-66):** they MUST be resolved with `params.environment.lock_index_strategy`; they MUST embed their own index URLs (`params.environment.lock_emit_index_url`); and they MUST cover the spec tooling as well as the runtime stack (`params.environment.lock_covers_spec_tooling`, `params.environment.pyyaml`). Rationale: `requirements.txt` carried the spec tooling alone while the W0 environment lived outside the repository, so nothing in the repository could rebuild the Python stack that produced its evidence — and SR-12 promised determinism against a lockfile that did not exist (AM-58). Each lockfile is resolved by `params.environment.lock_tool` at or above `params.environment.lock_tool_version_min` from its declared source, and MUST remain installable by plain `pip install --require-hashes` — the resolver is pinned so that *resolution* reproduces, not so the project acquires a runtime dependency on it (AM-61). *(verify: one documented command from a clean checkout that creates the environment, verifies hashes, materialises the SR-17 manifests, runs the data integrity and preprocessing tests, trains or resumes the W1 classifier and reproduces its validation metric within tolerance — all without reading a test sample; `tools/verify_cpu_lock.py --clean-install`; unit tests that learned-only metadata permits unavailable OpenJPEG, a mismatch fails, and J2K preflight rejects absence before artifact creation)*
